d}{K}^{2} & =K\\cdot K \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 16 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 16 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${K}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $K$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${K}^{6}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{6} & ={K}^{2}\\cdot {K}^{4} \\\\  & =O\\cdot {K}^{4} \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{K}^{6}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Power of a Nilpotent Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184160 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184064 :question/id #uuid "906ff9b7-c304-5b9a-b152-cef3bf343de1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4416" :question/problem "Find $C^5$ given that\n![](images/46/46d9e424cedc06da6c58d5219fc0161b29da17c75fa9dc714e758a0e3280b52a.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute $C^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $C^2=C,$ so $C$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute $C^5$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{5} & ={C}^{2}\\cdot {C}^{2}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C\\cdot C\\cdot C \\\\  & ={C}^{2}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & ={C}^{2} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](images/21/213331b37f44266f8bec1e00252499c88b2dc89114f93243a18dd2d1caaaddc9.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b51e8ff1-247b-5efd-a684-8fca6c064e1e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184765 :question/answe